==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun|premunires}}

# The [[offense]] under English law of bringing [[suit]] in or obeying a foreign court or authority, thus challenging the supremacy of the Crown. 
#*'''1724''', {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, ''[[s:The Works of the Rev. Jonathan Swift|The Works of the Rev. Jonathan Swift]]'', volume 9, [[s:The Works of the Rev. Jonathan Swift/Volume 9/The Drapier’s Letters 5|The Drapier’s Letters 5]]:
#*: For I have heard of a judge, who upon the criminal's appeal to the dreadful day of judgment, told him, he had incurred a '''premunire''', for appealing to a foreign jurisdiction; and of another in Wales, who severely checked the prisoner for offering the same plea {{...}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Diane Martin|chapter=Prosecution of the Statues of Provisors and Premunire in the King's Bench, 1377–1394|editor=J. S. Hamilton|title=Fourteenth Century England|location=Woodbridge, Sussex|publisher=[[w:Boydell & Brewer|Boydell Press]]|year=2006|page=109|isbn=978-1-84383-220-1|passage=William, angry that he had been forced to answer in an ecclesiastical court outside the realm of England, filed a suit of '''premunire''' against Richard Tylhe in the court of the king's bench during the Easter term of 1380, claiming £1,000 in damages. The statutes of '''premunire''' prohibited any Englishman from bringing suit in an ecclesiastical court if the cognizance, or jurisdiction, belonged to the king's courts.}}
# The [[writ]] [[charge|charging]] this offense.
#* '''1826''', {{w|Edward Coke}}; ''[[w:Edward Coke#Reports|The Reports of Sir Edward Coke, Knt. in Thirteen Parts. A New Edition, with Additional Notes and References, and with Abstracts of the Principal Points: The First Three Parts and the Fourth to Fol. 38a. by John Henry Thomas&nbsp;... the Rest of the Fourth and the Remaining Nine Parts by John Farquhar Fraser]]'', London: [[w:Joseph Butterworth|J. Butterworth & Son]], {{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/558018634 558018634]; reprinted as Clark, N.J.: The Lawbook Exchange, Ltd., 2008, [[Special:BookSources/9781584772392|ISBN 978-1-58477-239-2]], volume I, page 244:
#*: '''Premunire''' lies against an ecclesiastical Judge. Where a cause originally belongs to the cognizance of the ecclesiastical Court, although all circumstances being disclosed the cognizance belongs to the King's temporal Court; yet, if the suit is prosecuted there in the same nature as the cognizance belongs to them, no '''Premunire''' lies, but a Prohibition. But if in such case the Plaintiff sue in the nature of a suit which doth not belong to the Ecclesiastical Court, but to the Common Law, a '''premunire''' lieth.
# The [[penalty]] for this offense.

